From 70aff887b04e93b2d46f31847d86d4824813dacd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 08:18:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Harden the deployment: schema, session key, off-site backup
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three gaps that only showed up once this ran on a hosted server rather
than the office PC.

migrate_deals.py had to be run by hand and never was on Render, so
every Deals page was returning a 500 on "no such table: deal_agents".
Its work now lives in db.py alongside the other migrations and applies
itself on the next boot: the four commission columns, the deal_agents
table, and an idempotent backfill that describes existing deals and
makes each one's agent its sole 100% participant. No historic
commission amount is recalculated. The standalone script still works
for anyone who prefers to run it.

SECRET_KEY fell back to a constant published in this repo, which makes
every session cookie forgeable — a signed cookie naming any user id is
enough to be that user. It now takes the environment variable when set
and otherwise generates a key and keeps it beside the database. The
file is created with O_EXCL because the server runs several workers and
they must agree on one key, or cookies signed by one are rejected by
the next.

Backups all landed on the same disk as the database they protect, so
losing the server lost both. Settings gains an address to email a
weekly copy to, claimed atomically so only one worker sends it, with a
six-hour retry if the mail fails rather than a silent week's wait.

Two things found along the way: several workers booting an empty
database all inserted the first admin and all but one died on the
unique constraint, and that admin's password fell back to a fixed
string documented in the README. It is now generated and printed once
unless ADMIN_PASSWORD is set.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
